export const debate = {"id":"craig-grayling-evil-god-reasonable-2011","number":"98","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"William Lane Craig vs A. C. Grayling: Is Belief in God Reasonable in Light of Evil?","label":"Evil and divine reasonableness","date":"2026-08-28","duration":"1 hr 48 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vs7ArUMuQyg","motion":"Is belief in God reasonable in light of tsunamis, childhood cancer, moral evil, and the apparent harshness of the natural world?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Craig defends theistic reasonableness through freedom, epistemic restraint, and total evidence; Grayling presses suffering, better-world feasibility, ordinary moral knowledge, and secular morality.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"if it is even possible that human beings have free will","context":"Craig uses this modal qualification to deny that the hidden premises needed for a strict logical contradiction are necessarily true."},"con":{"text":"we go by our best lights and by what's most likely","context":"Grayling invokes fallible experience-based judgment to resist the claim that remote causal uncertainty disables ordinary moral assessment."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Theistic reasonableness","speaker":"William Lane Craig","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Secular objection","speaker":"A. C. Grayling","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":84,"con":86},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-logical-compatibility-and-freedom","title":"Logical Compatibility, Freedom, and Better Possible Worlds","timebox":"5:43–92:38","score":{"pro":88,"con":89},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-logical-hidden-premises","time":"5:43","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"The strict logical problem requires hidden premises connecting omnipotence and perfect goodness to the absence of evil, yet possible significant freedom prevents treating those premises as necessary truths.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The modal structure is exceptionally clear, separating a strict contradiction from the evidential question and identifying precisely why possible significant freedom prevents the required hidden premises from counting as necessary entailments. Principal limitation: The defense establishes compatibility only unless a richer account of omnipotence and perfect goodness independently entails those premises, so its success does not determine whether evil nevertheless makes theism improbable. Live burden: The opposing case must demonstrate necessity rather than intuitive plausibility, while this position must preserve the distinction between defeating contradiction and answering the actual world's evidential severity. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent coherence, precise qualification, central burden contact, and charitable representation, with only the acknowledged boundary between logical compatibility and broader probability limiting the move's reach.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-freedom-better-world","time":"30:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Bare compatibility does not show that a substantially better free world was infeasible; the theist must support the stronger modal claim that comparable good required this suffering.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling precisely isolates the modal burden, explaining that mere logical compatibility between freedom and suffering cannot demonstrate that an omnipotent creator lacked access to a better free world. Principal limitation: His interrogative formulation does not independently establish feasible actualization, since counterfactuals of freedom or valuable goods might constrain which improved worlds are realizable. Live burden: The theistic defense must substantiate its stronger necessity claim, while Grayling still owes engagement with sophisticated feasibility accounts rather than relying exclusively on the intuitive describability of freedom without terrible natural evil. Locked score: This exceptionally relevant, logically disciplined, charitable reply decisively contacts the hidden premise and appropriately limits its conclusion, although its evidential warrant remains less complete than its forceful burden placement, supporting the locked very-strong score of 88.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-significant-freedom-no-guarantee","time":"91:47","role":"Major direct reply","words":"God cannot causally guarantee that significantly free agents always choose rightly, so a world with comparable moral good but no moral evil may not be feasible.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: Craig directly distinguishes creating significantly free agents from determining their conduct, thereby supplying a logically coherent modal answer to the demand for guaranteed moral perfection without abandoning freedom. Principal limitation: The impossibility of causally fixing every choice does not establish that God could not actualize a comparably valuable world containing substantially less moral evil, and the reply leaves natural evil untouched. Live burden: Its carefully qualified feasibility claim must explain why omnipotent providence cannot select or sustain significantly freer circumstances with fewer atrocities while preserving responsibility and comparable goods. Locked score: The very strong 86-point result reflects decisive contact with both freedom and feasibility objections, explicit qualification, stable scope, and high relevance, while reserving deductions for incomplete warrant, charity, and the unanswered natural-evil challenge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-possibility-feasibility-gap","time":"66:09","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A free world without natural evil appears logically possible, shifting the decisive burden to an explanation of why such a world would nevertheless be infeasible for God.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling isolates the decisive modal gap, conceding that logical possibility does not itself establish divine feasibility while requiring why a free world without natural evil is infeasible. Principal limitation: The challenge identifies an unanswered burden because apparent consistency alone cannot exclude contingent constraints, counterfactual dependencies, or reasons that might restrict which possible worlds God can realize. Live burden: Grayling must show that freedom-based constraints do not extend to natural evil, while Craig must articulate a specific feasibility barrier instead of relying on the abstract observation that possibility and actualizability differ. Locked score: The very strong score of 90 rewards exact contact with Craig’s hidden-premise defense, near-complete responsiveness, stable qualification, and unusually charitable representation, while withholding top-band credit because the positive warrant for actual feasibility remains comparatively incomplete.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-evidential-evil-and-epistemic-limits","title":"Evidential Evil, Unknown Reasons, and Human Moral Knowledge","timebox":"12:30–55:55","score":{"pro":84,"con":89},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-epistemic-limits-providence","time":"12:30","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Finite observers cannot confidently infer that God probably lacks morally sufficient reasons for suffering because providential history, remote consequences, and causally relevant facts exceed their survey.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument precisely targets the disputed induction rather than denying suffering, emphasizing that finite observers cannot comprehensively inspect remote consequences, providential interactions, or every morally relevant feature needed for confident gratuitousness judgments. Principal limitation: Human incompleteness alone does not establish unreliability, and describing the difficulty as insuperable outruns the displayed support unless ordinary comparative moral inference is independently shown to fail here. Live burden: The defense must explain why unseen complexity materially weakens this particular probability estimate, while the objection must justify extending familiar local judgments to comprehensive divine-purpose assessments. Locked score: The strong upper-band placement recognizes direct central relevance, careful epistemic scope, coherent skeptical pressure, and substantial responsiveness, while retaining a meaningful deduction for overstated force and the unresolved bridge from limited knowledge to defeated comparative reliability.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unknown-reasons-insufficient","time":"50:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Unknown morally sufficient reasons cannot independently justify confidence in divine care when applied to tsunami drowning, painful cancer, and other severe harms whose alleged benefits remain unspecified.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling distinguishes the existence of morally sufficient reasons from having grounds to trust that such reasons explain tsunamis, childhood cancer, and gratuitous suffering. Principal limitation: His examples undermine claims that suffering benefits each victim, but do not fully exclude system-wide goods, benefits to others, prevention of worse outcomes, remote consequences, or independently grounded confidence in providence. Live burden: The defender must supply more than human ignorance to make divine care reasonable, while Grayling must address whether antecedent evidence for God could legitimately alter the evidential significance of otherwise inscrutable harms. Locked score: This coherent, centrally relevant, and charitable reply decisively contacts epistemic restraint and recompense, yet its incomplete treatment of broader unknown-reason strategies keeps evidential warrant below its strongest dimensions and supports the locked very-strong score of 88.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-ordinary-moral-knowledge","time":"54:08","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Fallible moral knowledge remains practically reliable despite remote causal uncertainty because experienced likelihoods still distinguish probable benefit from harm and prevent paralysis of judgment.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply offers a clear replacement standard for exhaustive foresight, grounding fallible moral judgment in experienced likelihoods and using the accessible ice-cream-versus-kicking comparison to resist practical paralysis. Principal limitation: Reliable local comparisons between immediate actions do not automatically license comprehensive probability judgments concerning every remote consequence of divine permission across history and interconnected causal systems. Live burden: The secular argument must bridge ordinary action guidance to the cosmic inference at issue, while skeptical theism must explain why uncertainty selectively disables that inference without undermining ordinary moral deliberation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding direct responsiveness, explicit probabilistic calibration, stable scope, high coherence, and strong defense of the central moral-knowledge bridge, with only the unresolved local-to-global extrapolation preventing an even more complete answer.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-divine-purposes-and-recompense","title":"Divine Purposes, Salvation, and Eternal Recompense","timebox":"15:38–91:50","score":{"pro":81,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-knowledge-god-eternal-recompense","time":"15:38","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Conditional on Christian theism, earthly happiness is not life's chief purpose; suffering may foster knowledge of God, and eternal fulfillment may outweigh temporal hardship.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move supplies an explicitly conditional Christian background model in which knowledge of God, freely received salvation, and eternal fulfillment alter expectations without claiming that suffering is intrinsically valuable or logically required. Principal limitation: A general non-hedonic purpose does not explain the extraordinary scale, distribution, and apparent indiscriminateness of childhood cancer, disasters, or suffering that destroys rather than develops agency. Live burden: The position must connect its doctrinal purposes to actual severe harms and clarify who receives the proposed benefit, while critics must distinguish reduced expectedness from complete justification. Locked score: The strong upper-band result reflects disciplined qualification, a coherent probability-adjusting structure, central contact with the theistic-context burden, and identifiable doctrinal support, tempered by limited evidence connecting those purposes to particular victims and magnitudes.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-religious-pluralism-salvation","time":"21:01","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Tsunami victims belonged to competing faiths, creating a pluralism problem if an exclusivist Christian salvation account denies many sufferers the relationship or recompense invoked to explain suffering.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling exposes tension between diverse-faith victims and exclusivist Christian salvation, contacting the salvific component of Craig’s divine-purpose and eternal-recompense response rather than reiterating suffering’s severity. Principal limitation: The reply leaves the operative doctrine unspecified and exclusion’s reach unestablished, while postponing development, so it cannot defeat inclusivist possibilities or answer the distinct claims about divine purpose and postmortem compensation. Live burden: Grayling must show that Craig’s explanation requires profession-dependent exclusivism, then demonstrate why alternative Christian accounts cannot extend salvation or recompense across earthly faith boundaries without becoming ad hoc or doctrinally incoherent. Locked score: At 78, this is a strong, relevant, appropriately tentative partial answer with meaningful doctrinal pressure, but its unstable scope, implicit qualifications, limited warrant, and incomplete contact with the broader target cap the result.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-providential-natural-disasters","time":"89:41","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Natural disasters may belong to a providentially coordinated plan in which complex contingencies serve kingdom-building and bring the greatest feasible number of people freely into relationship with God.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: Craig offers a providential structure rather than relying only on future compensation, connecting natural disasters, kingdom-building, divine omniscience, and freely formed relationship while explicitly presenting the account as a possibility. Principal limitation: the proposal remains speculative about how particular catastrophes advance that end, and omniscient capacity to coordinate contingencies supplies no comparative evidence that indiscriminate harms maximize free relationship rather than destroy agency, trust, or belief. Live burden: the theistic account must connect actual disasters to morally sufficient purposes and show why their observed distribution is preferable to less destructive alternatives, without converting hidden providence into an unrestricted explanatory license. Locked score: at 78, this is a relevant, coherent, appropriately qualified full answer, but its weak evidential bridge and incomplete particular-harm explanation keep it within the strong/competent band.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-future-recompense-blank-check","time":"55:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Future compensation cannot operate as unrestricted permission for earthly suffering, while the world's harsh facts reduce belief's reasonableness when included within the total evidence.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling integrates the recompense objection with evidential assessment, explaining that future compensation cannot independently transform earthly suffering into justified permission and that harsh realities diminish belief's reasonableness. Principal limitation: The reply comparatively underdevelops the crucial distinction between compensating victims afterward and possessing an independently sufficient reason for permitting their suffering, while offering limited argument about how strongly those realities outweigh Craig's wider evidence. Live burden: Grayling must demonstrate that eternal fulfillment neither accompanies adequate justification nor counterbalances the relevant evidential cost, whereas Craig must supply a nonarbitrary permission rationale rather than treating recompense itself as authorization. Locked score: The very strong score of 87 reflects exceptional motion-level relevance and decisive responsiveness alongside careful probabilistic calibration, but reserves credit because comparative warrant and charitable engagement remain materially thinner.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-divine-attributes-and-total-evidence","title":"Divine Attributes, the Actual World, and Total Evidence","timebox":"18:04–96:04","score":{"pro":87,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-within-total-evidence","time":"18:04","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Even conceding that evil counts adversely, the reasonableness of theism depends on all relevant evidence rather than on suffering considered as an isolated evidential datum.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument frames rational assessment as cumulative, concedes evil’s adverse evidential relevance, and directly connects the total-evidence standard to whether theistic belief remains reasonable. Principal limitation: Favorable evidence is merely referenced through unspecified published arguments, so the presentation neither supplies their premises nor comparatively weighs their probability against suffering, leaving confidence in a positive theistic balance materially under-supported. Live burden: Craig must demonstrate, rather than presuppose, that identifiable cosmological, teleological, moral, or historical considerations retain sufficient independent weight after the scale, distribution, and apparent gratuity of evil receive equally explicit probabilistic treatment. Locked score: At 85, this is a very strong, coherent, exceptionally relevant framing move whose qualification and charitable concession merit substantial credit, while the absent within-debate evidential comparison prevents a higher evaluation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-suffering-attributes-inference","time":"22:08","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"At first blush, tsunamis and childhood cancers indicate that an intelligent supernatural agent concerned with humanity would be either malevolent or materially limited in power.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: By grounding an explicitly preliminary inference in tsunamis and childhood cancers, the reply directly confronts providential and recompense defenses with concrete, morally consequential evidence about divine character and power. Principal limitation: The malevolence-or-limited-power disjunction remains preliminary rather than comparatively established, because perfectly good omnipotence could remain compatible with suffering if sufficiently specific, morally adequate reasons were independently supported. Live burden: Grayling must demonstrate that proposed freedom, soul-making, epistemic limitation, or eventual compensation explanations are improbable or insufficient relative to the observed severity and distribution of apparently gratuitous suffering. Locked score: The very strong 87 appropriately recognizes exceptional relevance, explicit qualification, stable scope, and decisive contact with both targets, while retaining deductions because the adverse inference does not yet exclude competing explanations.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-closing-cumulative-theistic-case","time":"94:57","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Logical compatibility, limits on confident providential probability judgments, Christian purposes, and the wider evidence together preserve the reasonableness of Christian theism despite evil.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The closing synthesis keeps logical compatibility, probabilistic assessment, Christian background purposes, and total evidence conceptually distinct while connecting them directly to the motion-level reasonableness claim. Principal limitation: Much of the case is recapitulated rather than comparatively demonstrated, and characterizing opposing probability judgments as enormously presumptuous exceeds what is needed to answer their strongest modest formulation. Live burden: The synthesis must show how its components jointly outweigh rather than merely survive evil, while the objection must avoid treating possible compatibility as the only standard under dispute. Locked score: The very strong band reflects comprehensive burden contact, exceptional motion relevance, coherent cumulative organization, stable scope, and substantial responsiveness across five objections, with the remaining deduction arising from compressed treatment of each answer and insufficient engagement with a cautious adverse inference.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-power-permission-dilemma","time":"24:59","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"The defenses appear divided between God's inability to prevent suffering while preserving freedom and God's ability to prevent it but permission for a greater good.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply connects three defenses by asking whether suffering reflects constrained divine power, permissive greater-good reasoning, or an appeal to mystery that weakens meaningful evidential assessment. Principal limitation: It treats those alternatives as inconsistent without establishing that they concern the same evil, identical modal conditions, and the same manner of prevention, thereby making the dilemma insufficiently exhaustive. Live burden: Grayling must show that freedom-based impossibility and greater-good permission are jointly asserted at one relevant level, rather than compatible explanations applying to different harms, choices, or counterfactual circumstances. Locked score: The strong 77 rewards outstanding responsiveness and central burden contact, but the reduced coherence, warrant, and charity ratings correctly reflect overstatement, missing qualification, and inadequate engagement with the strongest distinction.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-objective-morality-without-or-with-god","title":"Objective Morality and the Foundation of Judgments About Evil","timebox":"74:42–83:08","score":{"pro":81,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-objective-evil-moral-argument","time":"74:42","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"If objective moral values exist and cannot exist without God, then recognition that some suffering is objectively evil supplies a conditional argument for God's existence.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The explicitly conditional syllogism ingeniously transforms acknowledged objective wickedness from superficially countervailing evidence into affirmative theistic support, while preserving conceptual distinctions between genuine immorality and subjective disapproval. Principal limitation: Its indispensable metaphysical premise—that objective moral values cannot exist without God—receives considerably less argumentative substantiation than the deductive conclusion, leaving sophisticated secular-realist accounts substantially unanswered. Live burden: The reasoning must independently demonstrate divine necessity for moral objectivity, not merely establish widespread recognition of actual evil, and must explain why non-theistic normative foundations cannot provide comparably objective authority. Locked score: At 83, this remains a strong, logically coherent, precisely qualified, and highly responsive subsidiary reframe, with its evidential warrant and main-burden contribution constrained by the compressed foundation premise.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-human-needs-morality","time":"76:56","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Objective facts about sentience, relationships, security, interests, and vulnerability to suffering can generate moral demands without requiring a divine source of obligation.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies concrete, publicly accessible features of the human condition, including sentience, vulnerability, relationships, comfort, and security, as a substantive alternative to divine moral grounding. Principal limitation: Descriptive facts about needs and suffering do not yet explain why agents objectively ought to respond in prescribed ways, leaving the normative bridge stated more than defended. Live burden: The secular account must articulate how interests generate stance-independent reasons or obligations, while the theistic argument must show why that bridge requires God rather than merely questioning its completeness. Locked score: The strong upper-band result reflects direct contact with the divine-foundation premise, stable and concrete grounding facts, proportionate presentation, and practical evidential force, balanced by the unresolved metaethical transition from what sentient beings need to what moral agents objectively must do.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evolutionary-contingency-morality","time":"78:57","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Evolutionarily produced human attitudes do not automatically constitute objective morality, since differently evolved rational creatures might adopt radically different species-relative values and dismiss human norms.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The evolutionary-contingency thought experiment directly pressures human-needs morality by illustrating how differently evolved rational communities might endorse incompatible valuations and characterize supposedly universal norms as parochial species-preference. Principal limitation: The argument insufficiently distinguishes the causal genealogy of moral attitudes from their truth, justification, or reliability, so evolutionary influence alone cannot establish objective falsity or metaphysical nonexistence. Live burden: A successful diagnostic defeat requires an additional truth-tracking premise explaining why evolutionarily conditioned cognition is unreliable here, while addressing the possibility that adaptive capacities nevertheless apprehend independently valid normative truths. Locked score: At 78, this is a strong, relevant, and responsive challenge whose coherent contingency scenario materially tests secular objectivity, but whose logical bridge, qualification, and charitable engagement remain incomplete.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-suffering-constrains-choice","time":"80:58","role":"Major direct reply","words":"When one person confronts another's pain, the facts distinguish helping from further harm and provide a powerful experiential basis for mutual respect and kindness.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: Grayling’s vivid help-or-kick comparison makes the practical constraint imposed by another person’s suffering immediately intelligible, directly answers evolutionary contingency and divine-foundation challenges, and supports mutual respect without overstating its reach. Principal limitation: the example demonstrates compelling humane salience and motivational force more readily than stance-independent normative authority, because shared aversion to pain may guide choice without itself establishing an objective obligation binding every possible agent. Live burden: the secular account must explain why natural facts about suffering generate genuine reasons rather than merely widespread preferences, while preserving the reply’s careful claim that those facts seem to constrain action. Locked score: at 83, this is a strong, highly responsive, coherent, and well-calibrated subsidiary reply whose implicit modal bridge prevents promotion beyond the competent band.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":84,"strengths":["Separated the strict logical problem from the probabilistic problem and identified the necessity burden governing the former.","Developed a coherent epistemic-limits challenge focused on remote consequences, providential history, and finite observation.","Connected Christian purposes and eternal fulfillment to altered expectations about suffering without calling suffering intrinsically good.","Insisted correctly that the motion concerns the complete evidential balance, not evil considered alone.","Answered the better-world challenge by distinguishing significant freedom from causally guaranteed conduct."],"blunders":[{"text":"The positive evidence invoked by the total-evidence case was referenced rather than presented and comparatively weighed in the debate.","links":[]},{"text":"Providential explanations of disasters remained speculative about particular victims, actual effects, and the asserted maximizing relationship-building outcome.","links":[]},{"text":"The evolutionary contingency reply lacked the additional reliability premise needed to turn causal genealogy into a moral debunking argument.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":86,"strengths":["Distinguished a conceivable better free world from the stronger question of divine feasibility and repeatedly pressed the missing bridge.","Defended ordinary fallible moral knowledge through experienced likelihoods rather than demanding exhaustive causal foresight.","Converted tsunamis and childhood cancer into a calibrated adverse inference about combined divine power, goodness, and concern.","Challenged unknown reasons and future compensation as insufficient sources of positive warrant.","Offered sentience, needs, suffering, and humane response as a concrete secular basis for moral deliberation."],"blunders":[{"text":"The power-permission dilemma treated claims about different evils and different modes of prevention as inconsistent without establishing their shared scope.","links":[]},{"text":"The pluralism objection remained conditional on an insufficiently specified exclusivist salvation doctrine and answered only part of the theistic-purpose case.","links":[]},{"text":"The human-needs account did not fully supply the normative bridge from descriptive facts about suffering to stance-independent objective obligation.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"AI steelman: The existence of severe suffering can rationally count against theism without making Christian belief unreasonable when compatibility, calibrated epistemic restraint, morally serious divine purposes, and independently supported total evidence are assessed together.","premises":["Significant freedom can explain why God cannot guarantee universally right choices, although it does not by itself explain every magnitude of moral evil or any natural evil.","Finite observers should reduce confidence in judgments about comprehensive providential consequences, but ordinary moral knowledge remains reliable for condemning suffering and pursuing prevention.","A morally serious providential account must treat every sufferer as possessing noninstrumental value and cannot justify harm merely by citing benefits obtained by others.","Christian purposes and eternal fulfillment can reduce the unexpectedness of suffering only when joined to an account of restoration, consent, justice, and the preservation of personal identity.","Any final probability judgment must compare evil with independently defended evidence for God, rather than counting the mere demand for total evidence as evidence favoring theism."],"conclusion":"Accordingly, severe evil supplies genuine adverse evidence, yet Christian belief may remain reasonable if its independent evidence is substantial and its freedom, providence, and restoration claims satisfy these moral and explanatory constraints."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Victim-centered providence constraint","text":"A defensible providential explanation should include a victim-centered constraint: no person's extreme suffering may be treated solely as an instrument for someone else's faith or development. The theist can propose that permission is morally eligible only if the sufferer retains noninstrumental value, eventual restoration is personally continuous, and the relevant good could not be secured by a materially less harmful feasible route. This does not prove that actual suffering satisfies the constraint, but it makes the defense morally discriminating and creates clear conditions against which proposed reasons can be evaluated."},{"title":"Two-level epistemic restraint","text":"Epistemic restraint can operate at two levels without producing moral paralysis. At the practical level, agents should trust ordinary evidence, condemn apparent harms, aid victims, and prevent suffering because those are the best available responsibilities. At the comprehensive metaphysical level, the same agents may hold lower confidence about whether every permitted harm lacks any sufficient reason within an immense causal history. Separating these levels answers the ordinary-moral-knowledge objection while avoiding the stronger claim that ignorance itself establishes a divine reason."},{"title":"Comparative total-evidence protocol","text":"The total-evidence argument becomes substantive only through an explicit comparison. Assign evil genuine negative weight; distinguish moral evil from natural evil; state which independent considerations support God; and test whether those considerations predict a morally governed world better than relevant alternatives predict religious experience, moral awareness, contingency, and suffering together. The resulting case may favor, disfavor, or leave theism uncertain. Its advantage is methodological honesty: it transforms an appeal to absent published arguments into a transparent comparative burden that both sides can contest."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"AI steelman: Severe and apparently indiscriminate suffering provides significant evidence against an omnipotent, perfectly good, humanly concerned deity when proposed defenses lack specific predictive content and no feasible-world comparison explains the observed scale.","premises":["The argument need not show that God and evil are logically inconsistent; it needs only show that the observed pattern is less expected under the specified divine attributes than under relevant alternatives.","Ordinary moral knowledge provides defeasible evidence about suffering even when exhaustive remote consequences are unavailable, just as practical reasoning routinely operates under uncertainty.","A proposed divine reason should receive evidential weight in proportion to its specificity, independent support, and ability to distinguish the actual world from materially less harmful feasible alternatives.","Freedom explains why choices cannot be guaranteed, but it does not predict the magnitude of wrongdoing and does not directly account for earthquakes, cancers, or other natural harms.","Future restoration may compensate victims but cannot alone justify permission; justification requires an independently sufficient reason and a showing that less harmful means were unavailable."],"conclusion":"Therefore, unless the theistic account supplies independently supported reasons that predict or morally explain this pattern better than less harmful alternatives, severe suffering reasonably lowers confidence in the specified deity."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explanatory specificity test","text":"Unknown-reason defenses should be evaluated by an explanatory specificity test. A proposed reason gains weight when it predicts recognizable features of the world's suffering, explains why comparable goods could not arise through materially gentler means, and has support independent of the suffering it is invoked to explain. A reason loses weight when it accommodates every possible distribution equally well. This standard does not infer that no reason exists from our failure to identify one; it argues only that an indefinitely flexible possibility contributes little positive evidence to reasonable belief."},{"title":"Distribution-sensitive evil","text":"The evidential force of suffering concerns not only aggregate quantity but distribution. Harms fall on infants, nonhuman animals, isolated populations, and people whose agency or capacity for religious development is destroyed by them. A purpose centered on freely formed relationship should therefore explain why suffering frequently removes the very opportunities it is said to advance. Distribution-sensitive analysis strengthens the objection without claiming certainty: patterns that repeatedly frustrate a proposed purpose are less expected under that purpose than patterns carefully limited to preserving agency or preventing greater harm."},{"title":"Feasible improvement benchmark","text":"The better-world challenge need not describe a perfect world or prove every creaturely counterfactual. It can begin with modest feasible-improvement candidates: earlier warnings for disasters, biological systems with lower childhood-cancer incidence, or constraints preventing the most extreme consequences of wrongdoing while leaving ordinary choice intact. The theist may contest any candidate's feasibility or hidden costs, but a sequence of limited improvements creates a more tractable comparative burden than an evil-free utopia. The objection gains force when no specific connection between these extreme harms and indispensable goods is supplied."}]}},"year":2011};
